      "title": "Sick-leave accrual ratio for fee-for-service staff",
      "bodyHtml": "<h2>Initial ratio: 20% administrative equivalent</h2><p>Clinical credits × 1.20 ÷ 30 = sick-leave hours earned. The direct bucket receives 1.00 equivalent hour per credit and the administrative bucket receives 0.20. Thus 25 credits = 30 equivalent hours = 1 hour of sick leave; 0.75 credit = 0.90 equivalent hour = 0.03 sick-leave hour. The administrative equivalent affects accrual only for psychotherapy credit services and is already included in service pay.</p><p>H-code direct hours and automatically paid indirect hours enter the respective accrual buckets once. For Categories 2 and 3, one direct hour + 10/60 indirect hour = 7/6 accrual hours. Category 1 uses separately submitted actual indirect time. Do not apply the clinical-credit 1.20 multiplier to H-code hours. Additional actual paid indirect and support time is included without double counting. Reconcile to all actual hours worked and add any HFWA shortfall.</p><p>This remains protected paid sick leave, not a new vacation bank. It accrues without a probation waiting period and is paid using the highest currently eligible rate under the sick-leave pay policy. Payroll applies the HFWA calculation rules for the leave dates, using the currently applicable clinical per-credit or direct hourly base rate, excluding Tier 3 additions and other bonuses or differentials. Preserve existing balances and more generous earned terms. HFWA generally requires at least one hour per 30 hours worked up to 48 annually, with applicable carryover up to 48 hours; protected uses, reasonable notice, permitted documentation and nonretaliation requirements apply. Do not apply a generic 10-hour rollover cap or 30-day notice rule to protected sick leave.</p><p>ITSCO may update the ratio prospectively through the dated handbook appendix and corresponding payroll policy version, with notice and any required agreement. Preserve accrued balances and historical service calculations. AI documentation does not change the ratio automatically or remove pay for required review and correction. Actual work remains compensable even with a zero allowance. Any separate vacation/general PTO entitlement retains its existing protections.</p>"
